<!--
  Defines `sql_gateway_bootstrap`: the only account that holds access management.
  Mounted into /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/.

  WHY A SEPARATE IDENTITY, rather than granting the application account.

  The SQL gateway needs an account that can CREATE USER (to provision
  sql_gateway_writer and sql_gateway_ro) and SET DEFINER (the curated views are
  created with an explicit `DEFINER = sql_gateway_writer`). Both privileges arrive
  together with access_management.

  CLICKHOUSE_USER is the account the application runs as: rest, worker, billing and
  detector all authenticate with it in docker-compose.prod.yml. Granting it access
  management would mean a compromise of any one of those services could create
  further accounts and grant them anything, which is persistence well beyond the
  read and DDL access those services need. So the privilege lives on an account that
  only the one shot provisioning and migration containers ever use.

  The password comes from the environment via `from_env`, so it is never written
  into this file: the dev stack supplies a weak known default, and
  docker-compose.prod.yml refuses to start without a real one.

  Cloud deployments do not use this file. The chart provisions the gateway accounts,
  and the bundled ClickHouse admin already holds these privileges.

  Note for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so this block
  avoids them. A decorative underline here is what stopped the server booting once.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <users>
        <sql_gateway_bootstrap>
            <password from_env="SQL_GATEWAY_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD"/>
            <access_management>1</access_management>
            <networks>
                <ip>::/0</ip>
            </networks>
            <profile>default</profile>
            <quota>default</quota>
        </sql_gateway_bootstrap>
    </users>
</clickhouse>
